    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "6 chord",
      "body": "Add the major 6th above the root to a triad and you get a **6 chord**. C6 is C E G A. On a minor triad it is written **m6**: Am6 is A C E F♯.\n\n**Why it matters:** It is the sweet, swing-era sound of a final chord in old standards and Beatles songs. Pianists often use C6 in place of plain C.",
